The last node is the units digit, so recursion lets the call return from the end of the list first. A returned carry is added to the current node. If the carry is still left after the head, a new node is placed in front.

For `9 -> 9`, the last call changes the second `9` to `0` and returns carry `1`. The first node also becomes `0`, so the final carry creates a new leading `1`.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
struct ListNode
{
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode()
    {
        val = 0;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1, ListNode *next1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int addCarry(ListNode* node) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return 1;
        }

        int carry = addCarry(node->next);
        int sum = node->val + carry;
        node->val = sum % 10;
        return sum / 10;
    }

    ListNode *addOne(ListNode *head) {
        if (head == nullptr) {
            return new ListNode(1);
        }

        int carry = addCarry(head);
        if (carry == 1) {
            return new ListNode(1, head);
        }

        return head;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack. The list is updated in place.

Reversing the list makes the addition feel like normal addition from right to left. After adding the carry, the list is reversed again so the returned list keeps its original order.

For `1 -> 2 -> 9`, the reversed digits are `9 -> 2 -> 1`. The first digit becomes `0` with carry `1`, then the carry moves through the other digits and produces `1 -> 3 -> 0` after restoring the order. The input links are changed temporarily, but the final list is returned in forward order.

```java
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode() {
        val = 0;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1) {
        val = data1;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1, ListNode next1) {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
}
 */

class Solution {
    public ListNode addOne(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null) {
            return new ListNode(1);
        }

        head = reverse(head);
        ListNode current = head;
        int carry = 1;

        while (current != null && carry == 1) {
            int sum = current.val + carry;
            current.val = sum % 10;
            carry = sum / 10;
            current = current.next;
        }

        if (carry == 1) {
            ListNode extra = new ListNode(1);
            ListNode tail = head;
            while (tail.next != null) {
                tail = tail.next;
            }
            tail.next = extra;
        }

        return reverse(head);
    }

    private ListNode reverse(ListNode head) {
        ListNode previous = null;
        ListNode current = head;

        while (current != null) {
            ListNode nextNode = current.next;
            current.next = previous;
            previous = current;
            current = nextNode;
        }

        return previous;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the possible new node.

The list stores the most significant digit first, so the carry must travel to the end before changing earlier digits. The helper returns either `0` or `1`. A final `1` means the number gained a new digit at the front.

For a one-node list containing `8`, the helper changes it to `9` and returns no carry. For a one-node list containing `9`, it changes it to `0`, and the outer function adds a new `1` node.

