Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for recursive calls. The existing list nodes are modified instead of copied.

This avoids reversing the list. First, find the last node whose digit is not `9`. Increase that digit, then change every node after it to `0`. If no such node exists, all digits were `9`, so a new leading node is needed.

The edge cases are covered directly: an empty list becomes `1`, and `9 -> 9 -> 9` becomes `1 -> 0 -> 0 -> 0`.

```javascript
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    constructor(val = 0, next = null) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = next;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    addOne(head) {
        if (head === null) {
            return new ListNode(1);
        }

        let lastNonNine = null;
        let current = head;

        while (current !== null) {
            if (current.val !== 9) {
                lastNonNine = current;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }

        if (lastNonNine === null) {
            return new ListNode(1, this.setZeros(head));
        }

        lastNonNine.val += 1;
        current = lastNonNine.next;
        while (current !== null) {
            current.val = 0;
            current = current.next;
        }

        return head;
    }

    setZeros(head) {
        let current = head;
        while (current !== null) {
            current.val = 0;
            current = current.next;
        }
        return head;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the list is scanned a constant number of times.
Space complexity: `O(1)` besides the new leading node when all digits are `9`.

A stack stores the nodes in forward order, then gives access to the last digit first. Each digit is updated while the carry is `1`. If every digit was `9`, a small new node is added at the front.

Dry run for `1 -> 9 -> 9`: the stack processes `9`, `9`, then `1`. The values become `0 -> 0 -> 2`, so the result is `2 -> 0 -> 0`.

